Victor Danilchenko (danilche@cs.umass.edu) wrote:

> 	Hmmm... I was hoping it would work, but no such luck.
> 	I am having the same (I think) problem. I start Communicator, then I
> try to run a java applet (or simply open a JavaColsole window) -- and it
> starts spinning. If I just wait for half an hour, it finishes -- but, of
> course, I am not THAT patient. There is no link to java_30 in my
> directory. I tried having it access java40.jar by creating a link to it
> in ~/.netscape, or by setting MOZILLA_HOME. Neither one is an
> improvement.

I have this same problem.  I have gone so far as to hide all the
directories from netscape 3, and created a "virgin" user account to
make sure nothing in my environment was messing things up.  With
netscape 3 hidden and a default environment it still refused to start
up Java (I don't have as much patience as you I guess -- you waited a
half hour?!?).

> 	To add insult to the injury, the guy next desk runs Netscape4.04 with
> java fine! he is also using Digital Unix 4. I see no relevant
> differences in our setups, but there it is...

You're in a perfect position to track down the problem...  From my
tests, it seems like it's not a problem with version 3 files, and it's
not a problem with environment variables, so that pretty much leaves
different versions of shared libraries being the only really likely
culprit.  Here are the shared libraries used by netscape 4:

    /usr/shlib/libXm.so
    /usr/shlib/libXmu.so
    /usr/shlib/libXt.so
    /usr/shlib/libX11.so
    /usr/shlib/libXext.so
    /usr/shlib/libdnet_stub.so
    /usr/shlib/libm.so
    /usr/shlib/libots.so
    /usr/shlib/libc_r.so
    /usr/shlib/libc.so

Check to see if any of these libraries are different between your
machine and the guy in the next desk.  If they're all the same, then I
must say I'm stumped...   If you find out what the problem is though,
please let me know!

