I've run into an interesting problem...

In order to simplify upgrades of Digital UNIX on our various Alphas, we install
a "generic" (i.e., generic hostname and IP, only / and /usr in /etc/fstab)
Digital UNIX load, add all the keyboard, X and such drivers with setld, then
use "dump" to get root and /usr to tape, which is then installed on each
machine.

The idea is that we then "know" what the OS looks like on each system.

This "cloning" has worked through Digital UNIX 3.2G, but seems not to work for
4.0B. Specifically, we're finding that a GENERIC /vmunix built on our build
machine (use a 1000A and do "cd /sys/conf; doconfig -c GENERIC") won't boot
to multiuser mode on older 3000's. The system boots nearly up, but then panics
at the point it's about to (or is) launching X.

Worse yet, we've found an even wierder situation in that, if we boot to single
user mode on the 3000's with the following commandS:

	boot -fl s dka0
	fsck /dev/rz0a
	fsck /dev/rz0g
	mount -u /
	mount /usr
	doconfig

We cannot build a new /vmunix. DOCONFIG comes up with empty []'s for the
hostname and then core dumps whether you say "yes" to change hostname or "no"
to not.

I thought I had it fixed when I noticed a swap error, so after doing
"mount /usr", did

	swapon -a

to activate swap, but got the same core dump on DOCONFIG.

This same tape upgrades 255s, 1000As, 2100s, 4100s just fine.

Anybody else trying to do this? Any ideas on what's going wrong?

I'm faced with potentially doing the "regular" install, then sending out a
script to do all the setld's to load the remaining software (some of which
is on the additional software disks... starting to see why tape is easier?
We do the same thing on our IBMs, sending out a "mksysb" installable tape
of the "generic" OS and that works great).

Thanks for any input.
