villa wrote:
> 
> Hi,
> I need a tool to track malloc in my programs.
> A friend suggest me ATOM.
> I work with Digital Unix 3.2D on Alpha Stations.
> Where is it available?

The tool you are looking for is Third Degree, which
is a ready-to-use tool that is based on Atom.

Atom was not added to Digital UNIX until V4.0, but if
you would like our unsupported beta-quality Advance
Development Kit for V3.2* (which many people have
used for a couple of years), I can send you information
on how to get and use it.

If I may correct the misconceptions in the replies to
your entry:

> It is on the Digital UNIX operating system cd rom

This is only true for V4.0 and later, not V3.2*.

> ATOM was not obviously useable without
> a great deal of study.

Writing your own Atom tool does involve learning an API,
but using one of our ready-to-use tools just involves
looking at its man-page as for any other tool. To run
Third Degree, see the third(5) man-page. For example,
to start with you could type 

        atom -tool third <program>

and set the LD_LIBRARY_PATH environment variable to "." before
running the instrumented program and looking at its .3log report.

If you want to change the behavior of the tool, create a ./.third
file of commands (similar to a .dbxinit file) - details in third(5).
If you are just tracking malloc, you usefully write these commands
into ./.third

        uninit_stack no
        uninit_heap no

to turn off the memory checking.

        Thanks
                john



