OK, I made it through the basic setup and I think the NIC and software are OK
- it's just my weak configuration messing things up now.

I've gone through the netsetup - and I may have gotten somewhere, but
certainly not as far as I'd like.  The only thing I did differently with
'netsetup' that I hadn't already tried through the GUI or by manually editing
files is to use 'gated' instead of 'routed'....  Can someone explain the
relevance of these in terms of me wanting to hook this workstation up to the
local network?  I thought I read in routed man page that it should only be
used for RIP.

Anyway, changing to gated allowed me to issue 'ping' commands without getting
hordes 'address unreachable' or 'unknown host' messages - in fact, it just sat
there.  I can always ping 'localhost' as well as the static IP address
assigned to this machine - but that's all.

With gated, I configured my /etc/routes file ( which I was advised by DEC s/w
support to do ) with 1 entry -> "default    <gateway.ip.add.ress>".  The
'gateway' ip address is the address of our router, which connects us to the
internet.  This is apparently a 'static' ip address that is 'initialized'
during boot time.  Do I need other entries??

I'm not so much bothered by the fact that I haven't been able to get DNS up
and running yet -> it's only going to be a client, not running a DNS server ->
but it seems like I can't even get off that box onto the local network.  The
workstation also can NOT be 'ping'ed from anywhere.

Do I 'NEED' to have entries in the hosts.equiv?  It seems like this is a cheap
way to do rlogin.

If I need more /etc/routes entries, what should the 'Dest', 'Name1', and
'Name2' be:
Dest    Name1    Name2
----    -------    ---------
If the 'Dest' is a router, does that qualify as a 'host' or a 'network'??

Seems like I'm missing a one-liner from ONE of my config files...any help is
greatly appreciated as always!!!


