On this Digital UNIX V4.0B (Rev. 564) system, grep and egrep both
seem to absolutely refuse to do regular expressions, and I was sorta
wondering if anyone knew some trick to make them behave normally.

In checking with "man grep", I find that the RE stuff seems to have
been radically expanded, with things called BREs and EREs, and no
apparent clues as to how to tell the little beasties to enable one
or the other.  Is there some magical incantation that's needed to
make it work?

Here's proof of the problem, in a directory with lots of *.c files.
As you can see, there are several examples of 'fork' and 'Fork' in
the files, but '[Ff]ork' doesn't match anything. Using egrep gives
exactly the same output.

===> which grep
/sbin/grep
===> grep fork *.c
conn.c:* Connect two ports together.  This program forks, and each
process then
ping.c:                 perror("ping: fork");
tcpserver.c:* Wait for a connection.  When we get one, we fork off a
child to
root@ral1::jc/src/misc: grep Fork *.c
conn.c: D5("Fork ...");
conn.c: if ((pid1 = Fork()) <= 0) {
conn.c: if ((pid2 = Fork()) <= 0) {
ping.c:         if ((pid = Fork()) < 0) {
proxy.c:                if (Fork()) {
tcpserver.c:    if (child = Fork()) {
===> grep '[Ff]ork' *.c
===>

Tests with any REs other than literal strings always fail.  Patterns
like 'f.*k' and 'f*ork' also fail, though both should match the 'fork'
strings as shown above.



