Thank you all - we figured it out - with the help of a field service rep - and all
of your input of course.  Umm....it was the NIC card's config......we (myself and DU
Network Support People) set the card manually to the type of network we have using
the 'set ewa0_mode <something>' commands - tried several actually - but the one we
didn't try was 'auto' -> which did the trick today - autoconfig apparently.  With
that came the beautiful link light - oh yeah, the one back in my network patch panel
room - because SOMEONE decided not to include indicator LED's on the NIC card itself
- never seen it before until this Alpha came in the door - bravo to the product
manager!!

Quick question for future reference:  In reference to other buyers/admins direct
experience, is it common for the 'on board' NIC card to be preset to 'auto' for
autoconfigure, or does it come default as something else - like 'twisted-pair' (we
purchase this DU Alpha system from a VAR who configured some additional devices for
us)?  In this case, the card only had one connector - for twister-pair.  And is it
my responsibility, as the buyer of the system, to know about these nuances??  I'm
thinking this should not have taken so much effort - my fault for not being more of
a Unix guru....

Oh yeah, is there a way to get a list of all the possible values for all of the
'system variables' that are presented after executing a 'show' command at the '>>>'
prompt??  For instance, I'd like to know all the possible settings that are valid
for the NIC card ( set ewa0_mode <all_possible_values> )??

Thanks again everyone...we're rockin' now!!!

