In article <34ED8540.41C6@is.digital.co.kr>,
yongjoonchoi  <yongjoonchoi@is.digital.co.kr> wrote:
> In advfs or ufs, how to allocate quota per user(total 20,000) using
> shell script?

You could do any of these things:

1) Set up some quota prototype users with your standard quota(s), then
use

	edquota -p prototypeuser realuser

to apply prototypeuser's quota to realuser.

2) Write a program that uses the quotactl() call to set someone's quota.
It works for both advfs and ufs, even if your manpage says differently
(and it may).


#1 works pretty well if you only have a handful of default quota
configurations.  #2 works better in general, especially if you have a lot
of users that have odd quotas.

If you check the alpha-osf-managers mailing list archive, you'll find a
program in there by Richard Jackson that does what you need, if you decide
to choose option #2.

Tim
