In article <34EAFC6A.794B@eddie.mit.edu>, "John Chambers (from root
account)" <jc@eddie.mit.edu> wrote:

> On this Digital UNIX V4.0B (Rev. 564) system, grep and egrep both
> seem to absolutely refuse to do regular expressions, and I was sorta
> wondering if anyone knew some trick to make them behave normally.

> ===> grep '[Ff]ork' *.c

I just tried exactly this same command and it worked correctly.  Therefore
I would suspect your shell being guilty of not properly honoring the single
quota marks.

Try the following .c program to see if the arguments are being passed to
grep the way you expect them to

main(int ac, char **av)
{
    printf("%s\n",av[1]);
}

If your shell is not the problem, then you should see

% test_program '[Ff]ork'
[Ff]ork

If you do not see the string within the quote marks printed out as is, then
your shell is messing with the string before it is passed to the program
(in this case the test program, but in your case grep and anyother command
that accepts strings with []'s in them).

