On Mon, 9 Feb 1998, Andrew Ireland wrote:

> I;ve checked the obvious places, but cannot find out...
> 
> 
>     How do you limit the size of a core file?

With the ulimit command.  You can set both hard and soft limits with the
command, I recommend altering the soft limit.  This command will prevent
core files from being created:

	ulimit -Sc 0

You can change the 0 to whatever block size you want.  This particular
ulimit command is a shell builtin, at least in the Korn shell, so you'll
have to look at the ksh man page to get info on it.  (Tip, once you're
looking at the man page type a forward slash and then type "ulimit" to
go straight to the entry.)

