In article <6bonv7$qcg@loamer1>, yoda@ele.crl.melco.co.jp (Kiyoshi Yoda) writes:
>
>Greetings, I am using Digital UNIX 4.0a for my 21164, 500MHz clone machine (256MB RAM,
>600MB swap).
>I run a memory-eating program with the "top" and "swapon -s" memory checking codes.
>During the run, RAM capacity is quickly decreased and then system crashes. 
>At this stage there is huge swap space remained.
>
>Is there any suggestion ? 

Look at the crash dump listing.  The traceback should offer a clue
where it crash and thus why.  It might have nothing to do with the
amount of page/swap you have.  The crash dump file and the listing
are in /var/adm/crash.  You might also want to check for hardware
errors by looking at the error log.  You can use uerf(8) or dia(8)
to format it.  If dia(8) is installed, use it since it knows about
the newer systems.

