Jean-Claude Magras wrote:
> 
> I am contributing to a known freeware project and I am having trouble
> with strftime (on Digital UNix 3.2).. I use the "%T: meaning HH:MM:SS
> is the output string. I have checked the contents of the struct tm
> I pass in and they seem valid (that is the mt_hour/mt_min/mt_sec
> fields seem valid but strfime() usually reports "00:00:00". It
> sometimes puts an asterisk or slash in the hours field or
> occassionally a 12 or 3 but its never correct.

Here's some input from a strftime maintainer:

 I can't be sure what the problem is from this limited
description. I'm assuming he mis-typed the tm field names
(tm_hour, tm_min, tm_sec) in this message, but not in the
program itself. If he wants to use %T as the output format,
I assume his code looks something like this:

   . . .
   char dtbuf[MAXSIZE];
   time_t clock;
   struct tm *timeptr;
   
   clock = time ((long*)NULL);
   timeptr = localtime(&clock);
   strftime(dtbuf, MAXSIZE, "%T", timeptr);
   printf("%s\n", dtbuf);

Of course, as an i18n person, I should point out this is
not a good way to code things. He should get the output
format from a locale. I looked at the Digital locales, and
virtually all of them use the equivalent of %T for their
time format (t_fmt). That means he needs to include <locale.h>
and <langinfo.h>, add a call to setlocale() at the beginning
of the program, and call strftime() this way:

strftime(dtbuf, MAXSIZE, nl_langinfo(T_FMT), timeptr);

Then it should work.



