Message-ID: <34dde830.0@news.munich.netsurf.de>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 323180634
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


There seem to be plenty of unneeded writes to the system disk under
DUX, and under other Unices as well.

Any 'ls', 'cat', '/bin/sh' etc. updates the file's access time. The
net result is that there are writes to /usr, which are not really
needed. And writes to '/', of course.

Under some Unices one may mount /usr readonly, this requires a
separate /var. Under DUX this requires some reconfig of /usr, as for
building a new kernel etc. write access is required.

Though this is feasible, if would have the bad taste of being 'not
supported'. Updates might fail etc. I can do that with my station,
but not with a customer's system.

Inode updates in /tmp (which might/should be a link to /var/tmp) do
make sense, cause then a cron job can clean out old stuff.

Don't know what happens with directories, the problem might be
similar.

I think this would be unneeded overhead, especially in a multi-CPU
Turbolaser.

Are there any optimizations in AdvFS taking care of this? Does the
above 'problem' exist or am I missing something?

Thanks		Ruediger

