The problem is that with remote printer the 'if', 'of' 'xf', etc. won't
work.
I can print plain ASCII on my HP LaserJet with a simple trick I learnt
on 
the Linux Printing-HOWTO.
Look at my /etc/printcap

lj5m|lp4:\
        :lp=/dev/null:\
        :sh:\
        :lf=/usr/adm/lp4err:\
        :sd=/usr/spool/lpd4:\
        :if=/usr/lbin/lj5ps-remote:
lj5m-remote|lp5:\
        :lp=/dev/null:\
        :sh:\
        :rm=lj5m:\
        :rp=raw:\
        :sd=/usr/spool/lpd5:

This way if I want to print a PS/PCL, I address straight the remote
printer, using 'lj5m-remote'. No filters, all clean.
If, instead, I want to print ASCII, I use the 'fake' local printer lj5m.
As you can see, the lj5m entry is a trick to tell the system  that
printer
is local, so the filter /usr/lbin/lj5ps-remote will work. 
In my case the filter lj5ps-remote is a simple shell script: 

#!/bin/sh
/usr/lbin/ln03rof | lpr -h -Plj5m-remote

but you put what you prefer.

Of course, you have to put an entry lj5m-remote in your /etc/hosts!

If you have problem setting all this, e-mail me.

Massimo Gais
