Organization: Guest of OLEANE
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


This is a multi-part message in MIME format.
--------------917AB991C83E13703C230113
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit

Maybe you've been trapped in a problem I saw when on hire for a french
bank.
Our problem was that 'export-as' and 'import-as' directory were the
_same_dirs_ on the server, and mounts were done by _automount_.
And when mounting the service the first time, everything was ok, both on
real-server (ASE host #1 having the NFS server service) and others hosts
(ASE server - let's say #2 in same cluster or simple client).
But then, if the service goes to another host (ASE #2), the automounter
does'nt see this change and still wants to mount using a symbolic link.
If you don't need access to this dir. right now, it's ok for everybody
except for ASE #1.
And then, if the service is back on ASE #1, everybody will have problems
since :
- export dir is mounted (via symlink) from import dir, which is the
same.
- nfsd will try to export it, but may export only the symlink on
itself...
All this may be a little confused or errored (it's been a year I don't
work any more with DU nor ASE :-(, but the main idea is here.

So, if you encounter this type of problem (automount running on your ASE
servers, and export-as dirs and import dirs identical), your workaround
will be :
- use the automount -p option to force mounts over NFS (avoiding
symlinks mounts), this option was present only since DU 4.0x
- if running DU 3.2x, ask the Digital support services to recompile
automount from DU 4.0x under 3.2x (they've done it for use since we were
using DU 3.2g, I really regret the guys at DEC support).

Paul Robertson wrote:

> We are currently running Digital UNIX 4.0B on large
> AlphaServer 8400 machines currently running ASE 1.4.
> These servers serve both as compute engines and as NFS servers.
>
> We periodically (once every couple of weeks) have major
> problems with automount on these hosts, in that the automount
> process goes into state "U", and we are unable to access
> filesystems via NFS on these machines. The only thing that is
> peculiar about these hosts (as compared with the hundred or
> so well-behaved NFS client-only machines) is that the servers
>  use automount to access LOCAL filesystems under ASE. Note
> that NFS clients are still able to access disks mounted from
> the hung host (so the NFS server side appears OK).
>
> The problem appears to coincide with peak-load CPU usage on
> the host. However, even after the load subsides, the problem
> persists until reboot.
>
> Any ideas/experience appreciated.
