2 Ideas:
	1.) if it is a LPS print server, take a look at the "lpspr" command
	2.) Non-LPS printer, we grabbed a copy of "rprint" from Emulux corporation
and are using 
	rprint as an "of" qualifier in the printcap.  This rprint allows you enter
escape sequences
	into the printcap as flags, there are many other flags that can be added
as well.
		ex:
		laser2:\
			        :lp=/dev/laser2:\
			        :of=/usr/lbin/rprint:\
			        :sd=/usr/spool/lpd/laser:\
			        :emlx_n=256.256.256.1:\
			        :emlx_p=2501:\
			        :emlx_pcs=\033&l1O\033&k2S\033&l6C\033&l66P\033&l0E:\
			        :emlx_scs=\033E:\
			        :mx#0:\
			        :sf:\
			        :sh:
	I have only used it with TCP/IP, it would probably work with serial
printing but can not be of
	much help there.

Kylan

